window.BEGINNER_GUIDES = [{"title": "How the United States became independent", "question": "Why did the American revolutionaries need help from other countries?", "summary": "The United States began as a weak new country. Winning independence and surviving afterward required foreign help, money, and difficult choices about war and peace.", "sections": [["France helped make independence possible", "American forces could not rely on their own resources alone. France provided money, military help, and naval power because it wanted to weaken Britain. France was a monarchy, yet it supported a republican revolution: countries can cooperate because their interests overlap even when their political systems differ."], ["Foreign policy needs rules at home", "Independence left a practical problem: who could make promises to other countries and pay for them? The Constitution divided foreign-policy powers among institutions. Presidents conduct diplomacy, while Congress controls important powers over money and war, and the Senate has a role in treaties. A foreign promise depends partly on support at home."], ["Neutrality was a choice", "Neutrality means staying out of a particular war. In the 1790s, the United States still traded and negotiated with countries at war in Europe. Staying out required decisions about shipping, trade, and relations with Britain and France. Neutrality did not mean having no contact with the world."], ["Ideas and military help worked differently", "Republican ideas and Benjamin Franklin’s reputation could make the American cause attractive to foreign audiences. Armies, ships, and money changed the military balance more directly. Both mattered, but admiration alone could not win a war. Ask what each tool actually made possible."], ["Independence did not give everyone equal freedom", "The new republic’s language of freedom existed alongside slavery and the loss of Indigenous land and authority. A national success story can hide these unequal experiences. To understand the founding, compare what leaders promised with who gained rights, who remained excluded, and whose choices the new state restricted."]], "terms": {"Republic": "A political system without a hereditary monarch, in which public authority operates through political institutions.", "Alliance": "An agreement to cooperate, often for security or war.", "Neutrality": "A decision to stay out of a particular war.", "Recognition": "Another government’s acceptance of a state or government’s political standing."}, "home": "The new government had to divide power and find money for foreign commitments.", "abroad": "It needed help against Britain and later tried to avoid costly European wars.", "hard": "French ships, military forces, and material help changed what the revolutionaries could achieve.", "soft": "The appeal of republican ideas helped the American cause gain attention and support.", "steps": ["Open the 1778 Treaty of Alliance. Find one promise about military help.", "Open the Constitution. Find an institution with a role in making or funding foreign commitments.", "Write three sentences explaining why a promise abroad needs authority and resources at home. Do not apply the later Constitution to the original treaty negotiation."], "check": "Does an alliance prove that two countries share the same political values?", "answer": "No. France and the revolutionaries shared an interest in weakening Britain even though France was a monarchy. Cooperation can rest on shared interests as well as shared values.", "week": 1}, {"title": "Expansion, trade, and the Monroe Doctrine", "question": "How did gaining territory change both foreign relations and politics at home?", "summary": "The early United States expanded its territory and defended its trade. These choices also intensified arguments about presidential power, slavery, and Indigenous sovereignty.", "sections": [["More land brought new political disputes", "The Louisiana Purchase increased U.S. territory, but expansion was more than a change on a map. It raised questions about presidential authority and the future of slavery. New land could offer strategic advantages while creating new disputes over who would control it and how it would be governed."], ["A purchase treaty does not tell everyone’s story", "The Louisiana Purchase treaty records an agreement between governments claiming authority to transfer land. It does not prove that the people living there agreed to the transfer. Indigenous nations had their own political authority and interests. Reading the treaty carefully means asking whose consent and rights it leaves out."], ["Trade could become a tool of pressure", "The United States depended on trade across the Atlantic. European wars disrupted shipping and helped produce conflict that led to the War of 1812. Restricting trade was one attempt to pressure other countries without immediate military action. Economic pressure could also hurt people and businesses at home."], ["The Monroe Doctrine stated an ambition", "In 1823, President Monroe warned against further European interference in the Americas. That statement expressed a policy position, but the United States had limited ability to enforce every part of it. A public declaration shows what a government says it wants. Its military, economic, and diplomatic resources help determine what it can do."], ["Different tools produce different effects", "Buying territory, restricting trade, fighting a war, and issuing a public warning are different ways to influence events. A purchase involves bargaining. A trade restriction can impose costs. A warning may have little effect if others doubt it. Compare the tool, the target, and the result rather than calling every action simply ‘power’."]], "terms": {"Sovereignty": "A claim to rule a territory and make political decisions independently.", "Doctrine": "A stated set of principles that guides policy.", "Embargo": "A restriction or ban on trade.", "Capability": "The resources and practical ability to carry out a policy."}, "home": "Expansion affected constitutional debates, commercial interests, slavery, and Indigenous communities.", "abroad": "The government sought more territory and safer trade while resisting European interference.", "hard": "War and trade restrictions sought to change other countries’ choices by imposing costs.", "soft": "The image of an independent republic and claims of solidarity could appeal to audiences, but a declaration alone did not create influence.", "steps": ["Find a warning or principle in the Monroe Doctrine.", "Find a specific obligation in the Louisiana Purchase treaty.", "Explain the difference between announcing a policy and making an agreement. Identify what evidence would show whether either changed events."], "check": "Does announcing a doctrine mean a country can enforce it?", "answer": "No. A declaration states a position. Enforcement depends on resources, other countries’ reactions, and the wider international situation.", "week": 2}, {"title": "Expansion, slavery, and war with Mexico", "question": "Who benefited from U.S. expansion, and who paid the costs?", "summary": "Territorial expansion was closely connected to slavery, the removal of Indigenous peoples, and war with Mexico. More land did not mean equal benefits or lasting political unity.", "sections": [["Manifest Destiny helped justify expansion", "Manifest Destiny was a belief that U.S. expansion was natural or divinely favored. Supporters used it to justify acquiring land. The idea did not explain every decision by itself: interests in land, slavery, trade, and political power also mattered. Americans disagreed about both the goals and the costs of expansion."], ["Removal denied Indigenous peoples’ choices", "Official arguments for Indian removal sometimes presented it as protection or assistance. Such language can hide coercion and the loss of land and political authority. Indigenous peoples were political actors with their own institutions and strategies. A government’s description of a policy should be compared with the experiences of those affected."], ["War abroad sharpened conflict at home", "The Mexican-American War connected a dispute over territory and borders with arguments about the future of slavery. Gaining land raised the question of whether slavery would expand into it. Military victory therefore did not settle the political conflict. It helped create new domestic struggles over the character of the republic."], ["A peace treaty reflects unequal bargaining power", "The Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo formally ended the war and changed territorial control. A signed agreement establishes legal commitments, but it does not make the bargaining conditions equal. Its provisions about people and property must be studied alongside evidence about what happened afterward. The text alone cannot prove that promised rights were protected."], ["Success depends on whose interests we measure", "A country can gain territory while creating deeper political conflict and harming affected populations. To assess expansion, ask separate questions about land, security, rights, and long-term stability. These measures can point in different directions. A military victory is only one part of the historical judgment."]], "terms": {"Manifest Destiny": "The belief that U.S. territorial expansion was natural, destined, or divinely favored.", "Annexation": "The incorporation of territory into a state.", "Coercion": "Pressure that changes choices through threats, force, or imposed costs.", "Legitimacy": "The degree to which people consider power or a policy justified and acceptable."}, "home": "Expansion intensified disputes over slavery and the political balance between states.", "abroad": "The United States pursued territory through removal, bargaining, and war.", "hard": "Military force and unequal bargaining changed control of land.", "soft": "Claims about democracy and destiny sought support, but those claims did not make coercion legitimate to affected peoples.", "steps": ["Find how Jackson’s removal message describes the policy’s supposed benefits.", "Find one promise concerning people or rights in the Mexican peace treaty.", "Name a person or community whose experience you would need to check before accepting those claims."], "check": "Can a territorial victory create a serious political problem for the winner?", "answer": "Yes. New territory intensified disputes about slavery and political power. Gains on a map can coexist with conflict, injustice, and instability.", "week": 3}, {"title": "The Civil War and America’s growing power", "question": "Why did foreign governments matter in an American civil war?", "summary": "The Union and Confederacy competed for foreign support. After the war, industrial growth expanded U.S. resources, while disputes over rights and territory continued.", "sections": [["Recognition could change the war", "The Confederacy wanted foreign governments to accept it as an independent state. The Union worked to prevent that outcome. Trade, credit, and diplomatic support could affect each side’s prospects. European governments made their own calculations rather than simply following American demands."], ["Emancipation changed the meaning of the conflict", "The Emancipation Proclamation connected the Union war effort more directly to ending slavery in the areas it specified. This mattered to audiences abroad as well as within the United States. It made support for the Confederacy more politically difficult in some settings. The document’s actual scope still matters when evaluating its claims."], ["Military action could create diplomatic risks", "The Union blockade sought to restrict Confederate trade and resources. It also affected foreign shipping and commercial interests. A government fighting for survival still had to consider whether its actions might bring another power into the conflict. Military and diplomatic decisions therefore shaped one another."], ["Postwar expansion continued conflicts over sovereignty", "After the Civil War, U.S. authority and settlement expanded across the continent. Treaties with Indigenous nations recorded obligations and claims, but the written agreement did not tell the whole story of power or implementation. Reading a treaty requires attention to its parties, promised rights, and later consequences."], ["Industry created possibilities, not automatic decisions", "Industrial development increased resources that could support trade, transport, and military activity. Resources alone did not dictate a particular foreign policy. Political leaders, business interests, workers, and other groups contested how national capabilities should be used. Greater capacity created options, while politics shaped choices."]], "terms": {"Confederacy": "The seceding Southern states that fought the Union in the American Civil War.", "Emancipation": "Release from slavery.", "Blockade": "An attempt to stop movement or trade into or out of an area, usually using military force.", "Industrial capacity": "The ability to produce goods, transport supplies, and support large-scale activity."}, "home": "The war concerned the Union’s survival and slavery, followed by disputes over rights and expansion.", "abroad": "The Union sought to prevent recognition of the Confederacy and limit foreign intervention.", "hard": "Armies and the blockade restricted the Confederacy’s choices and resources.", "soft": "Antislavery claims could strengthen the Union’s appeal abroad, alongside strategic and commercial calculations.", "steps": ["Identify where the Emancipation Proclamation applied.", "Identify a promise in the Fort Laramie treaty.", "Explain why you would need evidence beyond each text to judge whether its promises changed people’s lives."], "check": "Did industrial growth make overseas expansion inevitable?", "answer": "No. It supplied resources and new possibilities. Domestic choices and international conditions still shaped how those resources were used.", "week": 4}, {"title": "America becomes an overseas power", "question": "How can one country control another without formally taking all its territory?", "summary": "Around 1898, U.S. power expanded overseas. Hawai‘i, Cuba, and the Philippines experienced different forms of control, and people debated empire both abroad and at home.", "sections": [["Overseas expansion took several forms", "The war of 1898 and its aftermath extended U.S. power beyond the continent. Annexation, occupation, colonial rule, and limits on another state’s independence were different arrangements. They imposed different obligations and restrictions. Understanding empire requires examining the specific form of control in each place."], ["U.S. law gives a U.S. official perspective", "The resolution annexing Hawai‘i shows how the United States legally claimed authority. It does not establish that affected people consented or erase their own political history. A legal text is evidence of a government’s action and justification. Other sources are needed to understand opposition and consequences."], ["Cuba illustrates limited independence", "The Platt Amendment placed limits on Cuba’s freedom of action and provided for U.S. intervention under specified conditions. Cuba’s formal independence therefore existed alongside substantial external restrictions. A country can retain its own government while another power constrains important decisions. Control does not always require direct annexation."], ["Claims of improvement could accompany coercion", "U.S. leaders sometimes described rule or intervention as bringing education, health, or better government. The Philippine-American War shows how those claims could coexist with violence and resistance. Calling a policy helpful does not establish how affected people experienced it. Ask who defined improvement and who could refuse it."], ["A larger role required more than military force", "Canals, naval access, diplomacy, financial arrangements, and interventions expanded U.S. influence. These tools did not all work in the same way. Diplomatic agreement could advance a goal without immediate war, while the surrounding imbalance of power still mattered. The practical terms of an agreement reveal more than its friendly language."]], "terms": {"Empire": "A system in which a state exercises power over other territories or peoples.", "Colonial rule": "Political control of a territory by an outside power.", "Intervention": "Involvement in another country’s affairs, sometimes using force.", "Formal independence": "Official status as a separate state, which may still face major external constraints."}, "home": "Debates concerned race, citizenship, commercial interests, and whether empire fit republican principles.", "abroad": "The United States sought strategic positions, markets, and influence overseas.", "hard": "War, occupation, and intervention rights limited other societies’ choices.", "soft": "Promises of progress and education sought acceptance, but attraction must be demonstrated rather than assumed.", "steps": ["Find a power claimed in the Hawai‘i resolution.", "Find a restriction in the Platt Amendment.", "Describe the difference between annexing territory and limiting another country’s decisions."], "check": "Does having an independent government guarantee freedom from outside control?", "answer": "No. Cuba shows how formal independence can coexist with restrictions and foreign intervention rights. The actual distribution of power matters.", "week": 5}, {"title": "World War I and the struggle over peace", "question": "Why could a president help design a peace settlement but fail to win support for it at home?", "summary": "The United States entered World War I in 1917. President Wilson proposed a new international order, but war aims and peace commitments met resistance at home and abroad.", "sections": [["Neutrality involved difficult choices", "Before entering the war, the United States remained connected to Europe through trade and finance. Maritime conflict and changing security concerns put pressure on neutrality. Americans disagreed about the costs of entering or staying out. Intervention was a political decision shaped by events, not an automatic outcome."], ["War required public resources and political authority", "The president could argue for war, but Congress had to authorize it and the country had to mobilize people and resources. Mobilization also brought pressure on civil liberties and political dissent. The ability to fight abroad depended on decisions at home. Domestic unity cannot simply be assumed from an official war declaration."], ["Wilson connected victory with a proposed new order", "The Fourteen Points set out principles for organizing peace. These included ideas about diplomacy and international cooperation. Such statements help reveal what leaders wanted to achieve. They do not prove that every group would benefit equally or that the proposals would be implemented."], ["International agreement still needed domestic support", "The Treaty of Versailles and the League of Nations involved commitments that faced opposition in the United States. Senate authority and partisan conflict mattered. A president bargains with other governments while also depending on support within the domestic political system. Success in one setting does not guarantee success in the other."], ["Rhetoric and results are different evidence", "Wilson’s language about self-determination appealed to many audiences, but its application was selective. To assess the policy, distinguish an announced principle from actual decisions and effects. Ask whose claims received support, whose did not, and what other interests shaped those differences."]], "terms": {"Self-determination": "The idea that peoples should have a say in their political status and government.", "Ratification": "Formal approval of an agreement through the required political process.", "Collective security": "An arrangement that treats threats to peace as a shared responsibility.", "Mobilization": "Organizing people, money, and production for a major effort such as war."}, "home": "Congress, the Senate, party conflict, and public debate shaped war and peace commitments.", "abroad": "The United States fought alongside partners and sought to influence the peace settlement.", "hard": "Military forces, finance, and supplies contributed to the war effort.", "soft": "Democratic language and peace proposals could attract support, but their credibility depended on application.", "steps": ["Find one reason for war in Wilson’s war message.", "Find one proposed peace principle in the Fourteen Points.", "Explain who would need to cooperate for that principle to work, including actors inside the United States."], "check": "Can a president promise international cooperation without securing support at home?", "answer": "A president can announce a goal, but lasting commitments depend on the relevant constitutional procedures, political support, and resources. The Versailles debate illustrates that limit.", "week": 6}, {"title": "Between the world wars and into World War II", "question": "Was the United States really isolated from the world between the wars?", "summary": "The United States avoided some military commitments while remaining involved in trade, finance, and diplomacy. World War II expanded its role and exposed contradictions between promises of freedom and domestic discrimination.", "sections": [["Staying out of an institution did not mean staying out of the world", "The United States rejected membership in the League of Nations but continued international economic and diplomatic activity. The label ‘isolationism’ can conceal these connections. A more useful question is which kinds of involvement Americans supported and which commitments they opposed."], ["War memories and economic crisis shaped choices", "The experience of World War I made many Americans wary of another major war. The Great Depression affected resources and political priorities. As threats abroad grew, leaders faced disputes over neutrality and assistance to other states. International danger mattered, but domestic views shaped the response."], ["The Four Freedoms linked security with values", "Roosevelt’s Four Freedoms described goals that went beyond territory or military victory. They presented freedom and security as connected issues for people at home and abroad. The speech is evidence of a political vision. Judging that vision also requires examining whom policy protected in practice."], ["Wartime rights were unequal", "Executive Order 9066 is central to studying the forced removal and incarceration of Japanese Americans. Its language of military authority must be read alongside what officials did and what affected people experienced. Universal promises of freedom did not prevent discriminatory treatment. This contradiction also matters for international credibility."], ["Winning together did not settle the peace", "The United States worked with allies whose systems and interests differed. Military cooperation could defeat a common enemy while leaving major disagreements about the postwar world. Plans for international institutions reflected hopes for cooperation, but institutions still depended on decisions, resources, and competing interests."]], "terms": {"Isolationism": "A label often used for opposition to foreign commitments, which can hide continuing economic and diplomatic involvement.", "Coalition": "A group cooperating for a shared goal, often temporarily.", "Civil liberties": "Basic freedoms that protect people from unjustified restrictions by government.", "Incarceration": "Confinement or imprisonment."}, "home": "War memories, depression, mobilization, and unequal civil rights shaped policy.", "abroad": "The United States moved from selective involvement to fighting the Axis and planning the peace.", "hard": "Industry, supplies, finance, and armed forces supported the war effort.", "soft": "The promise of freedom could attract support, while discrimination weakened its credibility.", "steps": ["Choose one freedom in Roosevelt’s speech.", "Find the authority described in Executive Order 9066.", "Explain why a statement of values and an account of actual treatment are both necessary to evaluate wartime policy."], "check": "Does rejecting a military commitment mean a country has no foreign involvement?", "answer": "No. A state may avoid one commitment while maintaining trade, loans, negotiations, and other international relationships.", "week": 7}, {"title": "The beginning of the Cold War", "question": "How did the United States try to limit Soviet influence after World War II?", "summary": "Containment aimed to limit the spread of Soviet or communist influence. Economic aid, alliances, military forces, and new institutions became parts of a growing U.S. security role.", "sections": [["Wartime cooperation gave way to rivalry", "The end of World War II left disagreements over security and political control. The United States and Soviet Union had fought a shared enemy, but their interests did not become identical. The early Cold War emerged through decisions and conflicts over the postwar order. Its exact form was not simply predetermined."], ["The Truman Doctrine connected local cases to a wider argument", "Truman sought support for assistance to Greece and Turkey in 1947. He presented those cases through a broader claim about resisting coercion and threats to free peoples. This helped justify action to Congress and the public. The broad language should still be compared with the specific conditions in each country."], ["The Marshall Plan linked recovery with security", "European recovery was an economic goal and a political concern. U.S. assistance could support production and stability while strengthening relationships and influence. Aid may create dependence, offer material incentives, or make a country seem a reliable partner. Those effects are different and need separate evidence."], ["Containment required institutions and resources", "Expanding commitments required organizations that could coordinate diplomacy, intelligence, and military planning. Alliances and the Korean War increased the military importance of containment. These choices had continuing costs and affected domestic priorities. Foreign policy became connected to permanent security structures at home."], ["Local actors had their own aims", "Conflicts described as part of a global rivalry also involved local histories and interests. Partners, governments, and movements could seek U.S. support for their own purposes. Applying the same explanation everywhere hides those differences. Containment changed across regions and over time."]], "terms": {"Cold War": "A prolonged rivalry between the United States and Soviet Union and their partners, including conflicts beyond direct war between the two superpowers.", "Containment": "Policies intended to limit the expansion of Soviet or communist influence.", "Aid": "Resources provided to another country or population.", "National security state": "Government institutions and practices organized around sustained security commitments."}, "home": "Anticommunism, congressional funding, and defense priorities supported and limited commitments.", "abroad": "The United States tried to stabilize partners and limit Soviet influence.", "hard": "Military alliances, weapons, and armed intervention supported containment.", "soft": "Recovery efforts and a reputation for reliability could attract cooperation, but aid could also work through material incentives.", "steps": ["Identify the problem Truman describes in his 1947 speech.", "Identify what Marshall Plan assistance was intended to change.", "Compare how military assistance and economic recovery might influence another country. Give a separate explanation for each."], "check": "Is all foreign aid an example of soft power?", "answer": "No. Aid can influence through money, conditions, or dependence. It contributes to soft power when it generates attraction or perceived legitimacy; that effect needs evidence.", "week": 8}, {"title": "Nuclear threats, new states, and credibility", "question": "Could the United States appear strong abroad while struggling over rights at home?", "summary": "Eisenhower’s administration relied heavily on deterrence while managing costs and global commitments. Decolonization and the civil rights struggle complicated claims about freedom and leadership.", "sections": [["Nuclear deterrence promised security at a price", "The New Look sought to maintain security without unlimited spending on conventional forces. Nuclear threats offered one way to discourage an opponent from acting. Such threats also created risks of escalation and doubts about whether they would be carried out. A cheaper military posture was not automatically a safer one."], ["New states did not simply choose a superpower’s agenda", "Decolonization meant the end of colonial rule and the rise of newly independent states. Their leaders and populations pursued their own security, development, and political goals. Viewing every movement only as pro-American or pro-Soviet misses those aims and the continuing effects of empire."], ["Suez showed that allies could disagree", "The Suez crisis demonstrates that U.S. power sometimes constrained allies rather than only opponents. Shared membership in a broad political camp did not eliminate conflicting interests. To explain a crisis, examine each government’s goals and choices rather than assuming one side acted as a single unit."], ["Civil rights affected international credibility", "The struggle at Little Rock concerned enforceable rights and the actions of Black students, families, activists, and officials. Foreign audiences also judged U.S. claims about freedom through events at home. Domestic rights were important in themselves, while their treatment also shaped the country’s reputation."], ["Eisenhower’s warning looked back on growing institutions", "The 1961 farewell address falls just after this week’s main period. It warned about the influence of a large military establishment and defense industry. The speech is useful as a retrospective source. It raises questions about how permanent security commitments affect democratic control."]], "terms": {"Deterrence": "Trying to stop an action by making its expected costs or risks too high.", "Decolonization": "The ending of colonial rule and struggles over what independence will mean.", "Credibility": "How believable an audience considers a promise or threat.", "Military-industrial complex": "The relationship between military institutions and industries that supply them."}, "home": "Defense spending, civil rights, and institutional power shaped security and reputation.", "abroad": "The United States managed nuclear rivalry, alliances, and relations with newly independent states.", "hard": "Nuclear threats, covert operations, and economic pressure sought to change behavior.", "soft": "Public diplomacy and claims about freedom depended partly on how domestic rights were protected.", "steps": ["Find the action authorized in the Little Rock executive order.", "Find the institutional danger described in the farewell address.", "Explain how protecting rights and controlling institutions connect domestic politics with international credibility."], "check": "Does an alliance mean its members agree on every crisis?", "answer": "No. Allies can pursue conflicting goals. Suez illustrates why their individual interests and choices must be examined.", "week": 9}, {"title": "Vietnam and the limits of military power", "question": "Why can a strong military fail to achieve a government’s political goals?", "summary": "Kennedy and Johnson expanded the tools available for Cold War competition. Vietnam showed how difficult it was to connect military action with a workable political outcome and lasting support at home.", "sections": [["More military options did not guarantee better decisions", "Flexible response gave leaders options beyond relying mainly on nuclear threats. Conventional forces and counterinsurgency appeared to offer more ways to respond. Yet having a tool does not establish that it can achieve the desired result. Leaders still needed a realistic account of the political problem."], ["Development programs depended on how people experienced them", "The Peace Corps represented a form of engagement centered on service and development. Such work could help build relationships and trust. Attraction was not automatic: recipients could view outside programs differently from their sponsors. To assess influence, examine local experiences and reactions."], ["Vietnam was more than a test of military strength", "Vietnam’s conflict involved colonial history, nationalism, ideology, and competing claims to political authority. Military activity could change battlefield conditions without settling those political issues. Counting operations or resources therefore could not by itself establish success. Local actors’ choices were central."], ["The Tonkin resolution expanded presidential authority", "Congress gave broad support for presidential action through the Gulf of Tonkin Resolution. The decision raises questions about information, delegated authority, and oversight. Formal authorization matters, but it does not prove the underlying judgment was sound. Students should separate the legal grant of power from the expected political result."], ["Domestic support had limits", "The draft, casualties, protest, and media scrutiny affected support for the war. Spending and attention devoted to Vietnam also competed with priorities at home. A strategy had to remain politically sustainable as well as militarily possible. Public consent could change as costs and expectations changed."]], "terms": {"Flexible response": "A strategy that offers several levels and types of response rather than relying on one kind of threat.", "Counterinsurgency": "Efforts to defeat an armed challenge to political authority, often combining military and civilian measures.", "Authorization": "Formal permission to act.", "Political outcome": "The change in authority, behavior, or relationships that a policy is intended to produce."}, "home": "The presidency, Congress, the draft, civil rights priorities, and protest shaped the war.", "abroad": "The United States sought to limit communist influence through force and development programs.", "hard": "Bombing and conventional military operations sought to impose costs and change the balance of force.", "soft": "Service, exchange, and development could build trust, but attraction could not simply cancel the effects of coercion.", "steps": ["Find an objective in the Peace Corps order.", "Find a power granted in the Tonkin resolution.", "For each, name one observable result that would show progress toward its goal. Avoid treating activity alone as success."], "check": "Does carrying out more military operations necessarily mean a policy is succeeding?", "answer": "No. Success depends on whether those operations help produce the intended political result. Military activity and political progress are different measures.", "week": 10}, {"title": "Negotiating with rivals in the 1970s", "question": "How can rivals negotiate without becoming friends?", "summary": "Détente aimed to manage competition and reduce some risks. The United States negotiated with the Soviet Union and opened relations with China while facing war fatigue and limits on presidential power.", "sections": [["Détente managed rivalry", "Détente means an easing of tensions. It did not end disagreement between the United States and its rivals. Negotiation offered ways to manage risks and commitments when resources and public support were under pressure. Cooperation on one issue could coexist with conflict on another."], ["Domestic trust affected foreign commitments", "Vietnam and Watergate damaged trust in executive claims. Congress and public opinion influenced what leaders could promise and sustain. Secret diplomacy might create room for negotiation while raising concerns about accountability. A commitment abroad still depended on authority and support at home."], ["China and the Soviet Union had their own interests", "Triangular diplomacy involved relationships among the United States, China, and the Soviet Union. Each government pursued its own goals and watched the others’ relations. None simply served as a passive tool of another. Other countries and communities also experienced the effects of these negotiations."], ["Diplomatic wording can preserve disagreement", "The Shanghai Communiqué illustrates the importance of distinguishing an agreement from an acknowledgment of someone else’s position. Deliberately careful wording can make cooperation possible without resolving every dispute. The Helsinki process also connected diplomacy with human-rights commitments that actors outside government could later use."], ["Negotiation is a method, not automatically soft power", "Talks may depend on military pressure, economic interests, or reassurance. Soft power concerns attraction and legitimacy. To evaluate détente, ask what changed, how it changed, and for whom. Avoid judging a decision only by outcomes that its makers could not yet know."]], "terms": {"Détente": "An easing of tensions between rivals.", "Triangular diplomacy": "Diplomacy involving the relationships among three major actors, here the United States, China, and the Soviet Union.", "Communiqué": "A public statement issued after diplomatic discussions.", "Ambiguity": "Wording that leaves more than one interpretation possible."}, "home": "War fatigue, economic problems, Watergate, and Congress constrained presidential choices.", "abroad": "The United States sought to manage competition through negotiation and changing relationships.", "hard": "Military capabilities and ongoing coercion formed part of the bargaining context.", "soft": "Reassurance and credible commitments could improve relationships, while negotiation itself did not prove attraction.", "steps": ["Find a shared statement in the Shanghai Communiqué.", "Find wording that describes one party’s position rather than a common agreement.", "Explain how those two forms of language allow cooperation while preserving disagreement."], "check": "Does negotiating with a rival mean accepting its political system?", "answer": "No. Governments can reach limited agreements while continuing to disagree about values, interests, and other conflicts.", "week": 11}, {"title": "Human rights and the end of the Cold War", "question": "Why is there no single simple explanation for the Cold War’s ending?", "summary": "Carter and Reagan combined values, security concerns, pressure, and negotiation in different ways. The end of the Cold War also depended on choices and changes outside the United States.", "sections": [["Human rights and security were intertwined", "Carter emphasized human rights, while regional crises and security concerns still shaped policy. Reagan emphasized confrontation and military strength while also negotiating. Treating one presidency as purely idealistic and another as purely strategic hides these combinations. The balance changed across issues and over time."], ["Domestic groups pushed policy in different directions", "Conservative movements, defense interests, human-rights advocates, and opponents of nuclear weapons sought to influence policy. Their competing priorities affected what leaders considered possible or desirable. Domestic politics could support confrontation, negotiation, or both. Public debate remained part of foreign policy."], ["Regional actors helped shape outcomes", "The Middle East and other regions had conflicts and interests that cannot be reduced to superpower competition. The Camp David process depended on choices by regional leaders as well as American diplomacy. An ally’s aims were not automatically identical to U.S. aims. Local histories matter when explaining an agreement or crisis."], ["A speech and a treaty provide different evidence", "Reagan’s Berlin speech reveals public language, priorities, and the audience he addressed. The INF Treaty records negotiated obligations concerning specified missiles and procedures. A speech cannot by itself prove that it caused a later event. A treaty’s promises must also be compared with evidence of implementation."], ["Several changes contributed to the ending", "Military pressure, diplomacy, domestic reform, and political change all belong in explanations of the Cold War’s ending. Gorbachev and other actors made consequential choices. A strong explanation identifies how a proposed cause worked and what evidence supports it. It should also address plausible alternatives."]], "terms": {"Human rights": "Claims that people have basic rights regardless of nationality or government.", "Arms control": "Agreements intended to limit or manage weapons and related risks.", "Verification": "Checking whether parties are carrying out an agreement.", "Causal explanation": "An account of how and why one factor helped produce an outcome."}, "home": "Political movements and disputes over defense, rights, and nuclear risks influenced policy.", "abroad": "The United States combined rivalry, regional diplomacy, and negotiations with the Soviet Union.", "hard": "Military spending, deterrence, and support for armed partners applied pressure.", "soft": "Claims about freedom and rights sought support, while their credibility depended on conduct.", "steps": ["Find one claim in Reagan’s Berlin speech.", "Find one obligation in the INF Treaty.", "Explain why different kinds of evidence are needed to assess a claim’s influence and a treaty’s implementation."], "check": "Does the fact that one event happened before another prove it caused it?", "answer": "No. Sequence is not enough. A causal argument needs a mechanism, supporting evidence, and consideration of other possible explanations.", "week": 12}, {"title": "U.S. power after the Soviet Union", "question": "What should a very powerful country use its power for?", "summary": "After the Soviet Union’s collapse, the United States had unusual global strength. It still faced difficult choices about war, humanitarian crises, trade, and international cooperation.", "sections": [["Greater power did not settle priorities", "The United States faced fewer constraints from a rival superpower, but Americans still disagreed about its role. Having the greatest capabilities did not make every goal achievable. Leaders needed to decide which interests mattered most and what costs they would accept. Other actors continued to make their own choices."], ["The Gulf War had a defined territorial objective", "The 1991 Gulf War concerned Iraq’s invasion of Kuwait and involved a coalition. Its authorization connected U.S. action with particular goals and international decisions. A limited military objective differs from rebuilding a state or transforming its political system. Clear boundaries help explain what success would mean."], ["Humanitarian crises raised hard choices", "Somalia, Rwanda, Bosnia, and Kosovo posed different problems involving violence, authority, and outside action. Support for intervention depended on perceived duties, risks, casualties, and the prospects of success. Intervention and nonintervention both had consequences. One formula cannot explain every decision."], ["Globalization created winners and losers", "Economic integration connected trade and investment with foreign-policy goals. It also affected jobs, industries, and political coalitions at home. Markets could create opportunities while distributing costs unevenly. Domestic disagreements over globalization were therefore part of foreign policy, not a separate issue."], ["Legitimacy still mattered", "Alliances, institutions, and public acceptance could make cooperation easier. They did not remove conflicting interests or guarantee support. Different audiences could judge the same action differently. Assessing the 1990s requires comparing goals and consequences across cases instead of giving the entire decade one score."]], "terms": {"Unipolarity": "A distribution of power in which one state has much greater capabilities than other states.", "Humanitarian intervention": "Outside action, sometimes military, justified as protecting people from severe harm.", "Globalization": "Growing connections across borders, including trade, investment, production, and communication.", "Mission scope": "The boundaries of what an operation is intended to accomplish."}, "home": "Trade disputes, jobs, congressional politics, and sensitivity to casualties shaped choices.", "abroad": "The United States supported an international order while responding selectively to crises.", "hard": "Coalition warfare, air campaigns, and sanctions could impose costs or stop actions.", "soft": "Democratic claims and international cooperation could attract support, but acceptance varied across audiences.", "steps": ["Identify the goal described in the 1991 authorization for force.", "Find a broader ambition in the 1999 National Security Strategy.", "Explain why a limited military goal and a broad international ambition need different measures of success."], "check": "Does being the strongest state mean being able to control every outcome?", "answer": "No. Capabilities do not eliminate resistance, local political problems, limited resources, or disagreement about goals.", "week": 13}, {"title": "September 11, war, and the search for a new balance", "question": "How did a response to terrorism become a long-lasting set of foreign and domestic commitments?", "summary": "The September 11 attacks changed security priorities and expanded government powers. Afghanistan, Iraq, and later diplomacy raised different questions about force, political goals, rights, and costs.", "sections": [["September 11 changed how leaders understood danger", "The attacks showed serious vulnerabilities despite U.S. military strength. Intelligence, coordination, and the ability to understand threats became central concerns. A government’s account of danger influences which actions appear urgent or acceptable. Threat judgments still require evidence and scrutiny."], ["Emergency authority could outlast the original decision", "The 2001 authorization permitted force against actors connected to the September 11 attacks and those who harbored them. Its later use raises questions about scope and oversight. A decision made in an emergency can shape policy for many years. Legal permission and strategic wisdom remain separate questions."], ["Afghanistan and Iraq require separate explanations", "The wars had different contexts, adversaries, and rationales. Counterterrorism seeks to disrupt threats, regime change seeks to remove a government, and state-building seeks to establish functioning institutions. These goals require different tools and evidence of success. Combining them does not make their requirements disappear."], ["Negotiation and pressure could work together", "The Obama administration faced inherited wars and sought to adjust priorities. The Iran case illustrates how sanctions and negotiations could operate within one policy. Sanctions work through economic costs rather than attraction. Diplomacy is a method of bargaining, whose results must be assessed through commitments and implementation."], ["Earlier choices constrained later choices", "Institutions, expectations, and ongoing commitments can make policy difficult to change. Leaders inherit costs and relationships rather than starting from nothing. To evaluate adjustment, specify what the government aimed to change and what remained. Include civilian effects, legitimacy, and long-term obligations in the assessment."]], "terms": {"Counterterrorism": "Efforts to prevent, disrupt, or respond to terrorism.", "Regime change": "The removal or replacement of a governing regime.", "State-building": "Efforts to establish or strengthen institutions that govern and provide public authority.", "Sanctions": "Restrictions intended to influence behavior by imposing economic or other costs."}, "home": "Fear, emergency powers, civil liberties, oversight, and war fatigue shaped policy.", "abroad": "The United States fought wars, pursued counterterrorism, and tried to adjust regional commitments.", "hard": "Military operations and sanctions applied force or material pressure.", "soft": "Cooperation and public engagement could build credibility, but their effects depended on conduct and reception.", "steps": ["Identify who the 2001 authorization permits action against.", "Identify a promised result in Obama’s 2015 Iran statement.", "For each document, separate the stated goal from the evidence you would need to show that it was achieved."], "check": "Are counterterrorism, regime change, and state-building the same goal?", "answer": "No. They seek different outcomes and require different capabilities. Progress toward one does not automatically achieve the others.", "week": 14}, {"title": "Understanding today’s foreign-policy debates", "question": "How can we study recent policy without confusing promises with results?", "summary": "Recent U.S. strategies offer competing views of national interests, alliances, and the economy. Because this period is still developing, conclusions must stay tied to dated evidence.", "sections": [["Recent history needs clear limits", "This week covers a 2017–2026 teaching horizon, not a finished account of the period. Selected strategy documents and media are snapshots. A policy announcement shows a position at a particular time. New evidence may change what can reasonably be concluded about its effects."], ["Strategy documents set out priorities", "The 2022 and November 2025 strategies describe national interests and proposed approaches. Comparing their language can show continuity and change. That comparison does not by itself establish what the government did or achieved. Implementation also depends on institutions, budgets, political support, and other actors."], ["The economy is part of foreign policy", "Supply chains, industry, jobs, and trade link domestic choices to international relationships. Cross-border dependence can create benefits and vulnerabilities at the same time. A policy intended to improve security may distribute costs unevenly at home. These effects help explain political disagreement."], ["Different forms of influence still need different explanations", "Military threats, sanctions, and technology restrictions apply pressure through different means. Alliances, education, and public diplomacy may help build trust or attraction. Labels such as ‘hard’ and ‘soft’ are useful only when we explain how the policy changes another actor’s choices. Nonmilitary action can still be coercive."], ["A current-affairs argument must be revisable", "Choose a recent claim and record the event date, publication date, and evidence used. Separate verified events, official statements, and your own interpretation. Compare the case with history without assuming the situations are identical. Explain what new evidence would make you revise your conclusion."]], "terms": {"Interdependence": "A relationship in which actors depend on one another, creating both benefits and vulnerabilities.", "Supply chain": "The connected steps and organizations involved in producing and delivering goods.", "National Security Strategy": "An official statement of a government’s security priorities and proposed approach.", "Implementation": "The actions and resources used to put a policy into practice."}, "home": "Elections, trust, industry, jobs, and institutions affect commitments abroad.", "abroad": "Governments debate how to manage competition, partnerships, and international cooperation.", "hard": "Military deterrence, sanctions, and technology restrictions can impose costs or limit choices.", "soft": "Reliable partnerships and credible institutions may attract cooperation, but outcomes need current evidence.", "steps": ["Choose the same topic in the 2022 and November 2025 strategies, such as alliances or industry.", "Write one similarity and one difference, with a reference to each document.", "Identify a dated budget, decision, or independent report needed to test whether those plans became action."], "check": "Can a strategy document prove that a government achieved its objectives?", "answer": "No. It establishes declared priorities. Evidence about actions, resources, and results is needed to assess achievement.", "week": 15}];
